%% prompt: 62. Building a city's tram network (prompts.md)
timeline TD
    title History of the Tram Network in the City of Port Halvern
    section Horse-drawn era
        1880s : The first horse-drawn trams begin carrying passengers along Harbour Street in 1884.
              : A second line soon links the fish market with the new railway terminus on the edge of town.
        1890s : The city council grants a franchise to the Halvern Street Railway Company, which promises to double the number of routes within five years.
    section Electric era
        1900s : Overhead wires replace the last of the horses, and the first electric trams glide silently through the old town.
        1910s : A grand central depot opens near the cathedral,<br>with room for more than one hundred tramcars.
        1920s : Tram ridership peaks as new suburbs spring up along the river and every neighbourhood demands its own stop.
    section Decline and revival
        1950s : Rising car ownership and a new bus fleet lead the city to close most of the outer suburban lines.
        1960s : The final tram of the original network makes its last journey, and crowds line the streets to say farewell.
        1990s : A heritage group restores two vintage cars and runs them on weekends along the waterfront.
    section Light-rail era
        2010s : Voters approve a bold plan to bring trams back, and the city begins surveying a route from the airport to the university.
        2020s : The new light-rail line opens to the public with twelve stations,<br>step-free platforms and trains that arrive every five minutes.
